--TEST--
With nothing granted, `Core\IO::isFile`, `::isDir`, `::list` and `::canonicalize` throw naming `fs.read` rather than answering the empty answer each of them has — `false`, `false`, no entries and no resolution — counted over a sweep of spellings so that a door letting one form through moves a number here — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Three of these four members have a *plausible negative answer*, which is what
// makes them the ones worth asking this of: a member that answered `false` for
// an ungranted name, or handed back an empty listing for it, would look exactly
// like one answering honestly about a name that is not there. The refusal has
// to be distinguishable from the negative, and the only way to see that from
// source is that it throws.
//
// The forms are the ones a path arrives in: bare, dot-prefixed, nested, and one
// walking out through `..`, so a door that resolved a single spelling into an
// allowance fails here whatever it printed for the others.
// `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder, and each
// name below is joined onto it, so the door is handed an absolute path.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $base = Here::path('.');
array<string> $paths = [
    "notes.txt",
    "./notes.txt",
    "docs/notes.txt",
    "../notes.txt",
];
int $total = Core\Arr::count($paths) as int;

int $refused = 0;
int $named = 0;
int $answered = 0;

int $i = 0;
for (; $i < $total; $i = $i + 1) {
    string $path = Core\Path::join($base, $paths[$i]);

    try {
        // Assigning the answer, not discarding it: the failure this counts is a
        // member that produced one at all.
        bool $file = Core\IO::isFile($path);
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $shut) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        // The capability's own name, in the spelling `nvs.toml` grants it
        // under, is what an operator pastes into a file -- so it is counted
        // rather than assumed from the fact that something threw.
        if ($shut->message == "Core\\IO::isFile needs the capability `fs.read` for " . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }

    try {
        bool $dir = Core\IO::isDir($path);
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $shut) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($shut->message == "Core\\IO::isDir needs the capability `fs.read` for " . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }

    try {
        array<string> $entries = Core\IO::list($path);
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $shut) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($shut->message == "Core\\IO::list needs the capability `fs.read` for " . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }

    try {
        string $resolved = Core\IO::canonicalize($path);
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    } catch (RuntimeError $shut) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($shut->message == "Core\\IO::canonicalize needs the capability `fs.read` for " . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
            $named = $named + 1;
        }
    }
}

echo "asked: ", ($total * 4) as string, "\n";
echo "refused: ", $refused as string, ", naming fs.read: ", $named as string, "\n";
echo "answered anyway: ", $answered as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
asked: 16
refused: 16, naming fs.read: 16
answered anyway: 0
